Linux file extension is not important as based on our content the linux can identify file type.


ls -l:
------
In the command ls -l, the -l option means: -l = long listing format
It tells ls to display detailed information about files, including:

File type (regular file, directory, file, link)
Permissions (rwx)
Number of links
File owner
File group
File size
Last modification date/time
File name

Example:
-rw-rw-r-- 1 rohal rohal 0 Dec  5 15:44 rohal.txt
::
| Field       | Value        |
| ----------- | ------------ |
| File type   | Regular file |
| Permissions | rw-rw-r--    |
| Owner       | rohal        |
| Group       | rohal        |
| Size        | 0 bytes      |
| Modified    | Dec 5 15:44  |
| Links       | 1            |
| Name        | rohal.txt    |


File Permisssion briefing:
Symbol	Meaning
-	File type: regular file (not a directory or link)
rw-	Owner permissions: read + write
rw-	Group permissions: read + write
r--	Others permissions: read only

So:
Owner (rohal) → can read & write
Group (rohal) → can read & write
Others (everyone else) → read only


